    {
      "@id": "urn:blue-lodge:standard:fifo-async-ipc-flowcontrol",
      "@type": [
        "DefinedTerm",
        "HowTo",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "FIFO Async IPC Flowcontrol & Garbage Collection Protocol",
      "description": "Zero-daemon, POSIX-compliant filesystem-backed asynchronous inter-process communication protocol using named pipes, atomic directory locks, sliding-window credit flow control, and single-pass AWK reconciliation.",
      "stability": "stable",
      "definition": "The official inter-process communication protocol for Blue Lodge agents, subagents, and tools. Eliminates daemon dependencies by leveraging kernel FIFO buffers, atomic mkdir locking, and credit tokens.",
      "articleBody": "Channels reside under .george/ipc/channels/<channel_id> containing rx.fifo, tx.fifo, config.json, state.json, keeper.pid, and lock.dir.\nMessages are formatted as single-line NDJSON frames strictly under PIPE_BUF (4096 bytes) for atomic kernel writes.\nFlow control is credit-driven: each DATA frame consumes 1 credit token. When credits reach 0, backpressure engages until CREDIT or RESUME frames arrive.\nReconciliation and reclamation are performed by ipc_gc (single-pass AWK engine) which terminates crashed keepers, frees wedged credit buffers, and removes expired promise files.",
      "step": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Open Channel",
          "text": "Call ipc_channel_open <channel_id> [window_size] [max_idle] to initialize pipes, keeper subshell, and state."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Send Frame",
          "text": "Call ipc_send <channel_id> <type> <payload_json> [uuid]. Writes atomically to rx.fifo under directory lock."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Read Frame",
          "text": "Call ipc_recv <channel_id> [timeout_secs] to pop the next inbound frame from tx.fifo."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Reconcile & Garbage Collect",
          "text": "Execute ipc_gc [--dry-run|--force|--ttl <secs>] to clean stale channels, expired promises, and wedged queues."
        }
      ],
      "codeSample": "source \"$LODGE_DIR/lib/ipc/fifo_ipc.sh\"\nipc_channel_open \"worker-1\" 5 300\nipc_send \"worker-1\" \"DATA\" '{\"action\":\"ping\"}'\nres=$(ipc_recv \"worker-1\" 5)\nipc_channel_close \"worker-1\""
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:blue-lodge:standard:okf-directory-structure",
      "@type": [
        "DefinedTerm",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "Blue Lodge OKF Hierarchical Directory Structure Standard",
      "description": "Architectural standard defining how directories and source code must be partitioned, named, linked, and documented in Google Open Knowledge Graph JSON-LD format.",
      "stability": "stable",
      "definition": "A standard requiring all subsystems to be organized by domain responsibility rather than flat aggregation, backed by machine-readable JSON-LD knowledge graphs, maintaining backward-compatible symlinks for existing POSIX sources.",
      "articleBody": "1. Domain-Driven Partitioning: Source files must reside in dedicated domain subdirectories (e.g. lib/core, lib/tools, lib/ipc, lib/sys, lib/comms, lib/memory, lib/agents, lib/ui, lib/knowledge).\n2. Backward Compatibility: For all modularized files, relative symlinks must be maintained at legacy entrypoints (e.g., lib/agent.sh -> core/agent.sh) to ensure zero regression for external callers and test runners.\n3. Semantic Graph Representation: Every directory, source file, exported function, and standard must have an entity node in okf/blue_lodge_graph.jsonld conforming to data/schemas/okf_graph.schema.json.\n4. Ephemeral Hygiene: Sandboxes, IPC channels, and temporary files must reside strictly in managed ephemeral directories with automated GC policies (ipc_gc, telemetry_gc), never cluttering root or configuration paths.\n5. Standard Protocols: All internal and external agent teams must reference and adhere to the OKF graph when discovering APIs, dispatching tools, or modifying subsystems."
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:blue-lodge:standard:polyglot-runtime-architecture",
      "@type": [
        "DefinedTerm",
        "HowTo",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "Blue Lodge Polyglot Runtime & Clean Backend Architecture Standard",
      "description": "Architectural standard defining language boundaries between the sovereign Bash harness, discrete Python domain backends, unified UV virtual environments, and containerized Rust/C++ execution.",
      "stability": "stable",
      "definition": "A standard forbidding multi-line inline language strings (e.g. python3 -c '...') in shell scripts. Enforces discrete domain backend modules, pure POSIX fallbacks for utility primitives, unified UV virtual environment management, autonomic FIFO IPC remediation, and hybrid tiered sandbox routing.",
      "articleBody": "1. Harness vs Domain Split: Bash is the sovereign harness and orchestrator (state, streams, agent loops, terminal UI). Complex data structures, semantic graphs, schema validation, and AST manipulations are delegated to discrete modules.\n2. No Inline Strings: Prohibits embedding multi-line python or rust inside bash strings. Discrete scripts must be co-located with their domain libraries.\n3. Unified Virtual Environment: Single authoritative virtual environment managed by uv at project root (.venv/) with zero-copy linkage to .george/venv to prevent split-brain drift and disk waste.\n4. Tiered Fallbacks & Remediation: Core utility primitives (SHA256, URL encoding) must provide 100% pure POSIX coreutils fallbacks. Complex backends detect missing packages, fingerprint incidents with SHA256, and dispatch autonomic remediation over FIFO IPC channels using uv.\n5. Polyglot Sandboxes: Python sandboxes leverage host uv for sub-second, hardlinked virtual environments. Rust and C++ sandboxes verify host cargo/gcc and transparently route to Docker containers when host toolchains are missing.",
      "step": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Co-locate Domain Backend",
          "text": "Extract complex Python logic into a discrete module within its domain directory (e.g. lib/knowledge/okf_backend.py) rather than inlining python3 -c."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Resolve Interpreter",
          "text": "Resolve Python via GEORGE_PYTHON, checking .venv/bin/python, uv run, or system python3."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Provide POSIX Fallback",
          "text": "Implement pure POSIX coreutils alternatives for utility functions (sha256sum, jq, awk) so baseline execution never hard-crashes without Python."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Autonomic FIFO Remediation",
          "text": "When optional runtime dependencies are absent, dispatch an autonomic remediation task via the FIFO Async IPC Protocol to self-heal using uv."
        }
      ],
      "codeSample": "# Resolve python and invoke discrete backend cleanly\npy_bin=$(_resolve_python)\n$py_bin \"$LODGE_DIR/lib/knowledge/okf_backend.py\" search \"query\" --json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:blue-lodge:standard:rbac-uuid-linking",
      "@type": [
        "DefinedTerm",
        "HowTo",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "Sovereign User Permissioning, RBAC & UUID Multi-Entity Linkage Protocol",
      "description": "Architectural security and identity protocol delivering RFC 4122 UUIDv4 generation, role-based capability bitmasks, and bidirectional multi-entity linkage graphs.",
      "stability": "stable",
      "definition": "The official authorization and identity linkage protocol for Blue Lodge. Enforces boundary isolation across sessions, subagents, and tools through cryptographic capability tokens and multi-dimensional UUID tracking (Session <-> Task <-> Agent <-> PID <-> Channel <-> User).",
      "articleBody": "1. UUIDv4 Generation: Every task, session, and channel must be identified by an RFC 4122 UUIDv4 string generated via kernel sysctl (/proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid) with an unprivileged POSIX AWK fallback.\n2. Role Hierarchy & RBAC: Four tiered roles (admin > operator > agent > guest) mapped to capability strings (shell:exec, workspace:write, workspace:read, tools:exec, agent:spawn, agent:kill, ipc:admin, ipc:read, ipc:write). Privileged operations must invoke auth_check_permission prior to dispatch.\n3. Multi-Entity Linkage Graph: Atomic relational graph stored in .george/identity/links.json mapping: session_id -> task_id -> agent_id -> pid -> channel_id -> user_id. Ensures full auditability, leak prevention, and autonomic orphan reaping.\n4. FIFO IPC Integration: All frames dispatched across named pipe FIFO channels must carry an explicit UUID task correlation header.\n5. Container & Sandbox Governance: Forms the foundational security kernel for subagent worktree and container sandboxing.",
      "step": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Generate Identity",
          "text": "Call uuid_generate to mint a unique RFC 4122 UUIDv4 token for tasks, channels, or sessions."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Enforce Capability Gate",
          "text": "Call auth_check_permission <subject_or_token> <capability> before dispatching privileged tools or modifying files."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Link Graph Entities",
          "text": "Call identity_link <sess_id> <task_id> <agent_id> <pid> <channel_id> [user_id] to record the relational binding in the identity graph."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "name": "Unlink on Completion",
          "text": "Call identity_unlink <key> upon task termination or channel closure to cleanly reclaim graph resources."
        }
      ],
      "codeSample": "source \"$LODGE_DIR/lib/core/auth.sh\"\nauth_init\ntid=$(uuid_generate)\nif auth_check_permission \"george-admin\" \"agent:spawn\"; then\n    identity_link \"sess_1\" \"$tid\" \"worker-1\" $$ \"ipc_chan_1\" \"george-admin\"\nfi"
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:blue-lodge:standard:subagent-sandbox-lifecycle",
      "@type": [
        "DefinedTerm",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "Subagent Worktree Isolation & Sandbox Lifecycle Standard",
      "description": "Protocol for spawning, monitoring, and reclaiming isolated subagent workers and containerized execution sandboxes.",
      "stability": "stable",
      "definition": "Governs the lifecycle of autonomous subagents. Requires separate git worktree branches or isolated Docker CUDA sandboxes, FIFO-based communication channels, strictly bounded turn ceilings, and guaranteed process reclamation on termination.",
      "articleBody": "Subagents must never execute destructive modifications directly on the parent working tree. Swarm workers run in isolated bash environments (or containerized CUDA sandboxes via Dockerfile.cuda-sandbox), communicate status via FIFO channels or subagents_update_status, and stream logs to .george/subagents/<sub_id>.log. Stale processes and worktrees are reaped automatically upon parent session termination."
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:blue-lodge:standard:telemetry-closed-loop-remediation",
      "@type": [
        "DefinedTerm",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "Autonomous Telemetry, Incident Preservation & Closed-Loop Remediation Standard",
      "description": "Deterministic error fingerprinting, scoped incident preservation, deduplication, and automated genetic self-healing (phytology) protocols.",
      "stability": "stable",
      "definition": "The standard protocol for capturing system failures without disk bloat, fingerprinting errors via SHA256 hashes, raising operator alerts, and queuing autonomous repair actions.",
      "articleBody": "1. Scoped Incident Capture: When preserving diagnostic incidents, large model binaries (*.gguf, *.bin) and root caches MUST be excluded from artifact snapshots to avoid disk bloat.\n2. Deduplication: Errors are indexed by sha256(class:subsystem:summary) to eliminate duplicate alert spam.\n3. Retention & GC: Incident snapshots must be aged out via telemetry_gc according to a 7-day retention window or 50MB per-incident limit.\n4. Closed-Loop Remediation: Striked operations undergo genetic self-healing via phytology auto-remediation before escalating to operators."
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:blue-lodge:standard:sovereign-inference-switching",
      "@type": [
        "DefinedTerm",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "Sovereign Inference Fabric & Dual-Profile Model Switching Standard",
      "description": "Protocol for orchestrating local multi-GPU vLLM/llama.cpp inference nodes and dynamically toggling between sovereign production and research profiles.",
      "stability": "stable",
      "definition": "Governs the PRISM inference fabric. Mandates strict split-GPU resource allocation (Dual RTX 3060 14,9 tensor split), SVD32 model compression, unified endpoint routing via endpoints.conf, and atomic profile switching via scripts/inference/switch_inference.sh.",
      "articleBody": "Tier 1 endpoints route to local PRISM server at port 8080 with 65k context. The active profile toggles seamlessly between Sovereign Production (Qwen3.8-27B-UD-Q4_K_S + SVD32 fusion) and Experimental Research (Champion-v5-Internal-MTP) without modifying application code or corrupting active sessions."
    },
